Subject: [PATCH] fix CGP-6cj7-7q33-776f: harden hyphenation PatternParser
 against XXE

com.itextpdf.layout.hyphenation.PatternParser.createParser() built a
JAXP SAXParserFactory (CWE-611 XML External Entity injection, with a
related CWE-776 entity-expansion DoS exposure) that only disabled
"http://apache.org/xml/features/nonvalidating/load-external-dtd". It
did not disable external general entities, external parameter
entities, or XInclude, and set no bound on entity expansion. Because
DOCTYPE declarations were still accepted, an attacker able to supply
or influence a hyphenation-pattern XML document (via
Hyphenator.getHyphenationTree, HyphenationTree.loadPatterns, or
PatternParser.parse and their FileInputStream-opening overloads)
could embed an internal DTD subset that declares an external general
or parameter entity, causing the parser to read local files, make
outbound requests (SSRF), exfiltrate data out-of-band via parameter
entities, or exhaust memory/CPU via unbounded entity expansion
("billion laughs").

The fix hardens the single chokepoint, createParser(), which is
shared by every caller that reaches parser.parse(source) (the
PatternParser constructor and getExternalClasses()): it explicitly
sets external-general-entities=false, external-parameter-entities=
false, keeps load-external-dtd=false, enables
javax.xml.XMLConstants.FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING to bound entity
expansion, and calls setXIncludeAware(false) to close the
independent XInclude fetch path. A blanket disallow-doctype-decl was
deliberately not used because 19 bundled hyphenation resources
declare a benign, subset-less "<!DOCTYPE hyphenation-info SYSTEM
hyphenation.dtd>" that must keep parsing without ever being fetched;
the chosen feature set rejects hostile DOCTYPE content while still
accepting that declaration. Any failure to apply one of these
features continues to propagate out of createParser() as a thrown
RuntimeException, so the parser is never returned under-hardened.

The regression test class
com.itextpdf.layout.hyphenation.PatternParserXxeTest drives the real
sink through the PatternParser constructor, parse(InputStream,String),
parse(String), and the public Hyphenator.getHyphenationTree(...) API
entry points. It asserts that the documented file-read payload
(file:///etc/passwd), a planted-secret file-read via both the
InputStream and FileInputStream-opening entry points, an SSRF
payload, and an out-of-band parameter-entity fetch are all rejected
with no external effect observed, that a billion-laughs bomb is
rejected promptly, that a raw XInclude-only payload with no DOCTYPE
does not resolve, and that the benign bundled DOCTYPE form and a
full bundled-style document driven through a caller-defined
IPatternConsumer still parse successfully.

